## 2. How Endpoint OS & Isolated Runtime Containment Works

1. **Host & Session Intake (`[100]` / `[200]`):** Requests and local/agent commands enter through governed ingress and host security checkpoints (such as firewall allow-list rules, identity/token checks, and per-session policy evaluation) before reaching isolated execution slots.
2. **Isolated Session & Resource Allocation (`[300]` / `[400]`):** Each active agent or tool session runs inside a dedicated isolated runtime environment (hardware-isolated microVM or sandboxed compute, CPU, memory, and filesystem) with explicit resource limits, session lifecycle controls, and scale-to-zero reclamation when idle.
3. **Boundary Enforcement & Cleanup (`[500a]`–`[500d]` / `[600]`):** Network egress and tool/code actions are constrained by allowlists, firewall/proxy rules, and isolated sandboxes, while session termination reclaims memory/compute and emits structured logs, metrics, and traces.

